<urn:usf:requirement:selective-check-owned-runtime-recovery:1> <urn:usf:predicate:text> "A killed check may retain its owned execution view. Exact canonical check recovery must authenticate all candidate manifest identities, current basis, preimages, scoped bytes, modes and non-runtime inventory while treating only the existing top-level .usf-check-tmp as operational runtime after verifying its current UID, mode 0700, non-symlink directory type and exact lexical/real path. Exclude its contents only after that boundary authentication; all other extra entries refuse. Ordinary candidate and public check-receipt readers remain strict. Share their unchanged algorithms through private check-recovery contexts, not a caller-controlled ignore list or relaxed ordinary guard. Read-only intent preflight returns its authenticated disposition; UNKNOWN refuses before cleanup. Reconcile graph-required current receipt members with retained intents before missing validation effects, then execute only missing obligations; derive ordering from observed dispositions, not fixed validation names. Matching current receipt reconciliation may settle and clear only the authenticated existing runtime scratch, then repeat ordinary strict receipt readback. A canonical attempt without a retained intent still requires ordinary strict workspace currentness before any cleanup or execution. Retained invalid, stale, unsafe or unknown state is preserved. No runtime contents establish candidate inputs, evidence, completion or authority." <urn:usf:graph:cli-transition-definition> .
<urn:usf:contract:cli-transition-definition:1> <urn:usf:predicate:constructionRouteConstraint> "The separately required owned-runtime check recovery extends the selective-check composition exception only in src/realisation/index.ts, src/commands/check.ts, src/commands/work-completion.ts and their already declared focused tests. It may factor existing workspace and receipt authentication into private strict versus exact check-runtime contexts; keep ordinary exported readers strict. Add named check-recovery readers and an authenticated no-change callback under the existing candidate lock, with no caller-supplied runtime paths or ignore flags. Check-local tree projection must authenticate the owned scratch root before exclusion. Native intent preflight may expose its existing authenticated disposition for dynamic recovery ordering. Both native entrypoints preserve unknown outcomes before cleanup; matching current receipt settlement may use existing scratch cleanup followed by ordinary strict readback. Preserve all other inventory, manifest, identity, mode, confinement, receipt schema/digest and lock guards. Tests must cover real retained execution views after SIGKILL, zero duplicate children, wrong scratch type/mode/symlink, unrelated extra entries, and ordinary-reader refusal. No new scheduler, provider, completion, parallel or metrics authority is admitted." <urn:usf:graph:cli-transition-definition> .
